### Common Environmental Archetypes

For your world-building, these are the "real" categories scientists use instead of just "Earth-like":

* **Super-Venuses:** High-mass planets with intense volcanic activity and runaway greenhouse effects (e.g., Gliese 667 Cc). [[26:45](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejg7fAbmlf4&t=1605)]
* **Steam Planets:** Hotter water-rich worlds where the ocean and atmosphere blur into a supercritical fluid. [[42:13](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejg7fAbmlf4&t=2533)]
* **Airless Rocks:** The most common outcome for planets orbiting close to active stars. [[01:13:24](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejg7fAbmlf4&t=4404)]
* **Mini-Neptunes:** Planets that look Earth-sized but are actually composed of deep gas layers. [[01:00:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejg7fAbmlf4&t=3612)]

### Sources for your Reference

* **Direct Imaging/JWST Data:** The primary source for atmospheric ruling (Trappist-1, LHS 1140 b).
* **Radial Velocity & Transit Photometry:** Used to calculate density and mass-to-radius ratios.
* **Bayesian Modeling:** Used to infer compositions when direct data is sparse. [00:17:24 in previous video]

